Using Terraform on Microsoft Azure, I deployed:

A fully segmented Virtual Network (𝟭𝟬.𝟬.𝟬.𝟬/𝟭𝟲) with dedicated subnets:

- Users (10.0.10.0/24)

- Admin (10.0.20.0/24)

- Guest (10.0.50.0/24)

- DMZ (10.0.60.0/24)

- Firewall + Bastion subnets


𝗔 𝗰𝗲𝗻𝘁𝗿𝗮𝗹𝗶𝘇𝗲𝗱 𝗔𝘇𝘂𝗿𝗲 𝗙𝗶𝗿𝗲𝘄𝗮𝗹𝗹 𝗲𝗻𝗳𝗼𝗿𝗰𝗶𝗻𝗴:

- Controlled outbound internet access

- Strict isolation of guest traffic from internal resources

- DNAT rules exposing only the DMZ web service

- Route tables forcing all traffic through the firewall (no direct internet breakout)


🌐 𝗪𝗼𝗿𝗸𝗹𝗼𝗮𝗱𝘀 𝗱𝗲𝗽𝗹𝗼𝘆𝗲𝗱

𝘋𝘰𝘮𝘢𝘪𝘯 𝘊𝘰𝘯𝘵𝘳𝘰𝘭𝘭𝘦𝘳 (𝘞𝘪𝘯𝘥𝘰𝘸𝘴 𝘚𝘦𝘳𝘷𝘦𝘳)

→ Backbone for identity and authentication

𝘋𝘔𝘡 𝘞𝘦𝘣 𝘚𝘦𝘳𝘷𝘦𝘳 (𝘓𝘪𝘯𝘶𝘹 + 𝘍𝘭𝘢𝘴𝘬 𝘢𝘱𝘱)

→ Public-facing application behind firewall NAT

𝘎𝘶𝘦𝘴𝘵 𝘞𝘪𝘯𝘥𝘰𝘸𝘴 𝘝𝘔

→ Simulates untrusted user environment

𝘈𝘻𝘶𝘳𝘦 𝘉𝘢𝘴𝘵𝘪𝘰𝘯

→ Eliminated direct RDP/SSH exposure


🔐 𝗦𝗲𝗰𝘂𝗿𝗶𝘁𝘆 𝗱𝗲𝘀𝗶𝗴𝗻 𝗱𝗲𝗰𝗶𝘀𝗶𝗼𝗻𝘀

Instead of flat networking, the environment enforces:

- Network segmentation aligned with security zones

- Default route → firewall (forced inspection)

- Guest network completely isolated from internal subnets

- Public access only through controlled NAT rules


This mirrors real-world enterprise patterns:

𝘋𝘔𝘡 𝘦𝘹𝘱𝘰𝘴𝘶𝘳𝘦 𝘸𝘪𝘵𝘩𝘰𝘶𝘵 𝘪𝘯𝘵𝘦𝘳𝘯𝘢𝘭 𝘤𝘰𝘮𝘱𝘳𝘰𝘮𝘪𝘴𝘦 𝘳𝘪𝘴𝘬


📊 𝗠𝗼𝗻𝗶𝘁𝗼𝗿𝗶𝗻𝗴 & 𝗩𝗶𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗶𝗹𝗶𝘁𝘆

Integrated Azure Monitor with a Log Analytics Workspace:

- Firewall logs (network, application, DNS)

- Centralized telemetry collection

- Foundation for SIEM integration


🧠 𝗪𝗵𝘆 𝘁𝗵𝗶𝘀 𝗽𝗿𝗼𝗷𝗲𝗰𝘁

The focus was to move beyond “deploying resources” and instead design:

- A secure-by-default architecture

- Infrastructure that reflects real enterprise constraints

- A foundation for identity, monitoring, and threat detection

